    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `Lazy.mount_composed_widgets`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `mount_composed_widgets` function is an asynchronous method designed to integrate a list of widgets into the parent widget's composition. It first checks if the parent widget exists; if not, it simply returns early, doing nothing. If the parent is present, it asserts that the parent is indeed a widget, ensuring type safety. The function then defines an inner asynchronous function `mount` that performs the actual mounting of the widgets. This inner function calls `parent.mount` with a replacement widget and positions it after the current widget. After mounting, it removes the current widget from the composition. The `mount` function is scheduled to run after the next refresh cycle using `self.call_after_refresh`, which is a helper method that ensures the operation is performed at the right time. This approach is useful for deferring widget updates until the UI is ready to refresh, improving performance and avoiding unnecessary intermediate states. The function does not return any value and handles the absence of a parent by returning early, thus avoiding unnecessary operations. It relies on the `mount` method of the parent widget and the `call_after_refresh` method of the current widget.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
